1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,048
JAMIE: SentinelOne beat earnings and dropped twelve percent anyway—which is the stock market's way of saying 'we loved your numbers, we hate your future.'
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/29/sentinelone-s-stock-earnings-ai-layoffs.html

2
00:00:09,048 --> 00:00:36,571
ALEX: So the S&P closed at seven thousand five hundred eighty-eight, up a third of a percent, Nasdaq at twenty-seven thousand and one, also up a third of a percent—which means the market is running on the thinnest possible margin of hope. Meanwhile the Russell two thousand dropped zero point seven seven percent, which is the small-cap equivalent of getting left at the gas station while everyone else carpools to the AI conference.

3
00:00:36,571 --> 00:00:42,227
JAMIE: So the rotation into mega-cap is still happening. Who were the actual winners today?

4
00:00:42,227 --> 00:01:12,012
ALEX: Salesforce up nine point one percent—that's the biggest gainer—Oracle up eight point three three, Microsoft up four point zero nine. Adobe up five point eighty-three. Basically if your stock has 'cloud' or 'AI' somewhere in the investor deck, you made money. Meanwhile Costco dropped four point seven four percent, Walmart down three point three one, Eli Lilly down two point nine eight. The consumer is either broke or the market has decided to hate grocery stores. Pick one.

5
00:01:12,012 --> 00:01:22,946
MAYA: Wait, Costco is down nearly five percent? That stock is like the boomer version of a growth play. That's the Crocs IPO of retail—it's not supposed to move.

6
00:01:22,946 --> 00:01:39,158
ALEX: Exactly. The V I X dropped one point five two percent, everyone's sleeping soundly, the soft landing narrative is holding. But the minute small caps started bleeding out, nobody panicked, nobody even looked. This is what peak indifference to inequality looks like.

7
00:01:39,158 --> 00:01:44,814
JAMIE: So Costco chose violence today, and nobody noticed because Salesforce had a good quarter.

8
00:01:44,814 --> 00:02:02,157
ALEX: SentinelOne beat earnings and then immediately told investors deal cycles are lengthening and customers are deferring purchases. That's not guidance—that's a corporate panic button disguised as a forecast. The stock tanked twelve percent because earnings don't matter if the next quarter looks like a recession.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/29/sentinelone-s-stock-earnings-ai-layoffs.html

9
00:02:02,157 --> 00:02:11,960
JAMIE: But wait—if deal cycles are lengthening, that means enterprises are still buying, they're just taking longer. That's not a demand problem, that's a timing problem.

10
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:35,336
ALEX: Except 'deal cycles are lengthening' is Wall Street speak for 'our customers are scared and our sales team can't close anything.' SentinelOne's guidance is basically 'somewhere between growth and contraction.' They're hedging so hard they might as well have said 'we have no idea what happens in ninety days.' The business works, the market just doesn't believe in the future anymore.

11
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:55,695
MAYA: So they beat on the past and got punished for the future. This stock has commitment issues. It's like saying 'I made great money last year, but I'm genuinely unsure if I'll make money next year,' and the market goes 'okay we're out.' How many other security companies are seeing the same thing?

12
00:02:55,695 --> 00:03:10,777
ALEX: The whole cybersecurity space is basically pricing in a spending freeze right now. Investors aren't asking 'is the product good?' anymore. They're asking 'will the CFO actually sign the check?' and the answer from SentinelOne was 'we're not sure.'

13
00:03:10,777 --> 00:03:26,989
JAMIE: So this is the real test of the soft landing narrative. If cybersecurity—which is supposed to be non-discretionary, recession-proof spending—is getting deferred, then we're not in a soft landing. We're in a 'companies are acting like a hard landing is coming' landing.

14
00:03:26,989 --> 00:03:52,250
MAYA: Exactly. SentinelOne didn't lose a customer. It just lost the market's faith that it will gain any new ones. And if security spending is optional now, nothing is mandatory anymore. Wikipedia editors are threatening to strike over AI training data—and I mean the people who literally built the internet's most trusted source are about to unionize against the thing that's supposed to make them obsolete.

15
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:58,282
JAMIE: Wait, how many editors are we talking about here? This isn't like a fringe group.

16
00:03:58,282 --> 00:04:17,888
MAYA: Hundreds of prolific editors—the people who actually maintain the thing. And the issue is that Wikimedia started letting AI companies train on Wikipedia content without compensation or even a heads-up to the people who wrote it. So basically the institution said 'we're monetizing your unpaid labor' and the volunteers said 'no.'
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/report/939442/wikipedia-editors-protest-wikimedia-layoffs-strike

17
00:04:17,888 --> 00:04:36,739
ALEX: So Wikipedia chose to become the training data for every large language model while the people maintaining it get nothing, and now those people are saying they'll delete their contributions if it doesn't stop. That's the digital equivalent of a factory worker threatening to dismantle the machines they built.

18
00:04:36,739 --> 00:04:54,460
JAMIE: And here's the thing—Wikipedia is one of the last places on the internet where you can actually trust the information. If the editors walk, the whole thing becomes just another dataset, not a community project. Wikimedia basically bet that the editors had nowhere else to go.

19
00:04:54,460 --> 00:05:04,640
ALEX: Gas prices are dropping—but they're still high, which is the economic equivalent of 'we fixed it but didn't fix it.' Consumer relief theater with an asterisk.
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/05/29/gas-prices-dropping-iran-summer

20
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:14,819
MAYA: So people are supposed to feel better because gas went down from the peak, but it's still brutal. That's not a win, that's just less losing.

21
00:05:14,819 --> 00:05:25,753
JAMIE: Trump's one point seven seven billion dollar anti-weaponization fund just got blocked by a federal judge—which means the administration chose violence today and lost in court before lunch.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/29/trump-doj-fund-lawfare-blocked-judge.html

22
00:05:25,753 --> 00:05:36,687
ALEX: The fund was temporarily blocked. So the whole thing is on hold. This is what happens when you try to turn the government into a political piggy bank.

23
00:05:36,687 --> 00:05:51,391
MAYA: And the timing is wild because they're trying to do this while simultaneously arguing they're not weaponizing the government. The judge basically said 'you can't call it an anti-weaponization fund if it's literally just paying off political allies.'

24
00:05:51,391 --> 00:06:06,096
ALEX: Fed Governor Michelle Bowman just warned against hiking interest rates on top of the inflation spike—and here's the thing: she's a governor actively arguing against the Fed's own playbook, which means the institution is fracturing in real time.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/29/feds-bowman-warns-against-hiking-interest-rates-due-to-inflation-spike.html

25
00:06:06,096 --> 00:06:21,554
JAMIE: So Bowman is saying energy prices and tariffs are driving inflation, and hiking rates won't fix that—it'll just crush growth. And she's right. But the fact that a Fed governor is publicly dissenting this hard means the consensus is breaking.

26
00:06:21,554 --> 00:06:38,520
MAYA: Infinity Ward just announced the next Call of Duty is set in a North Korean invasion scenario. So basically Activision looked at the current geopolitical situation and decided what the market really needed was a playable version of the thing that might actually happen.

27
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:56,618
JAMIE: Wait, so they're launching a game about North Korea invading while actual tensions are spiking? That's either the most tone-deaf product launch in gaming history or the most prescient—either way, Activision is betting that geopolitical instability is a feature, not a bug. They're selling anxiety as entertainment.

28
00:06:56,618 --> 00:07:21,879
ALEX: The game is supposed to be grounded in military authenticity, which is corporate speak for 'we did just enough research to sound credible while making sure you can still quickscope a general.' Activision spent millions making sure the weapons feel real and the scenario feels plausible, but the actual geopolitical context? That's just ambient flavor. It's like selling a recession simulator and calling it 'educational gaming.'

29
00:07:21,879 --> 00:07:54,303
MAYA: So in six months, if tensions actually escalate, Activision gets free marketing because millions of people have already run through the scenario in their living room. And if nothing happens, they just sold a game where you get to feel like a soldier. Either way, they're not losing sleep—they're just monetizing your fear of what might be coming. So we're going to hear 'strong execution, uncertain outlook' from every CFO for the next twelve months, and investors will just keep selling on the news.

30
00:07:54,303 --> 00:07:59,582
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
